<!--
    Launcher glyph: the PiKit wordmark, drawn as pixels.

    The mark is the approved pixel wordmark: thirteen cells wide and four cells
    high, with half-cell gaps between the letters. The path is the union of the
    occupied cells, not a redraw; the P's bowl is a real hole, so the path uses
    evenOdd. At 2.1 units per cell the mark is 54.6 by 16.8 units in the 108-unit
    adaptive-icon layer, with its bounding box on (54, 54). Those margins are
    8.7 units left and right and 27.6 above and below; the launcher shows the
    centre 72 units, so the wordmark keeps the proportions of the approved icon.

    **White, on black, with no colour of its own.** The monochrome treatment is
    deliberate: at 48-64dp a single flat shape is what reads, the icons around it
    are monochrome, and a tinted glyph would be the only coloured thing in the
    row. The themed-icon layer (`ic_launcher_monochrome`, which the system
    recolours) is the same picture as this one, so the icon is recognisable in
    either mode. Amber remains an interface accent rather than a logo: the setup
    screen's progress bar is the place it still appears.

    `tools/render-icon.py` derives the README's copy of this icon from this file
    and `values/colors.xml`, and `tools/build-apks.py` runs it in check mode, so
    the picture at the top of the README cannot drift away from the app's icon.
    (No double hyphen in an XML comment: the check flag's name is spelled out in
    that script's own docstring instead, because `aapt2` refuses a comment
    containing one.)
-->
